# Article: Project

Edit this file for your project. Every role reads it; keep it exact.

## Language and layout

- Language: <e.g. C# / .NET 9>
- Source layout: <where production code and tests live>

## Commands

These slots are split by *cost*, and roles depend on the split. `Unit
tests` is what every role runs between steps and before every handoff,
so it must be fast: if it needs Docker, containers, a browser, or a live
service, it belongs under `Acceptance suite` instead. Bundling them is
expensive and silent — every "keep the suite green" step in the
constitution starts paying end-to-end prices, and mutation testing,
which re-runs the suite once per mutant, stops being feasible at all.

- Unit tests: <fast command — no containers, browsers, live services>
- Coverage: <command, and where the report lands>
- Acceptance suite: <slow/end-to-end command, or "none yet">
- Lint/format: <command, or "none">

## Quality bars

See `docs/quality-bars.md` in swarm-forge-herdr: universal bars are the
**fast suite only**. Coverage, CRAP, duplication, mutation, format, and
acceptance are owned by named roles. Listing them here as universal
makes every handoff re-run the expensive battery.

Universal — these gate EVERY acceptance and handoff, whoever judges
(pack roles and the squad-leader alike). Because they gate every
handoff, they name the fast command only:

- <e.g. all unit tests green: exact fast command>

Role-specific additions:

- Cleaner: run duplication/complexity tools to *act* on findings; do
  not treat those numbers as the slice's official evidence.
- Hardener: fresh coverage, CRAP, duplication, mutation (survivors
  justified). These are the official metric receipts.
- QA: fast suite + acceptance suite + hands-on vs the specification.
  Do not re-collect coverage or re-run hardener metrics.

